# A specialized sibling of agent_network_instructions_editor.hocon: instead of taking a
# free-form "create or modify instructions" request, it always takes a failing-test report
# for an EXISTING network. For each failure it decides whether the NETWORK's instructions are
# wrong (fixes only the agents responsible -- instructions/description only) or the TEST's own
# expectation is wrong (corrects that one fixture via ANTeGen's own validate/persist tools).
# When a failure needs an agent added, removed, or rewired, that structural change is delegated
# to agent_network_designer, which reassembles and persists the network itself -- this network's
# own preserve_source_hocon persistence only ever patches instructions/description text.
# Unlike agent_network_instructions_editor (which is called as a sub-network of
# agent_network_designer), this network is driven directly by the `ns consultant` command. Its
# ConsultantDefinitionMiddleware loads the target definition, and ConsultantPersistenceMiddleware
# validates and saves source-preserving changes back to the same HOCON file.

{
    include "registries/aaosa_basic.hocon",
    include "registries/agent_network_instruction_improver.hocon",
    "metadata": {
        "description": "Fixes an existing agent network so that its failing generated tests pass. Given the network's hocon file and a report of which fixtures are failing and why, it decides per-failure whether to fix the responsible agent's instructions/description, correct that test fixture (only when the test's own expectation is wrong), or -- only as a last resort -- delegate the required add/remove/rewire to agent_network_designer. If the user's stated direction asks to reduce token usage, it also runs a token-reduction pass: flattening unneeded hierarchy, removing redundant agents, shortening verbose instructions/descriptions, and suggesting (never implementing) coded-tool conversions.",
        "tags": ["experimental", "tool"],
        "sample_queries": [
            "Fix generated/coffee_shop_customer_support_assistant based on its failing tests"
        ]
    },

    include "registries/expertise_scoping_instructions.hocon",

    # Load the shared LLM configuration from a single source of truth.
    include "config/llm_config.hocon",
    "max_steps": 500,
    "max_execution_seconds": 1800,
    "error_formatter": "json",
    "error_fragments": ["Error:", "Exception:", "Traceback (most recent call last)"],

    "tools": [
        {
            "name": "consultant",
            "function": {
                "description": "Diagnose failing tests and fix the responsible network instructions, incorrect fixtures, or required structure.",
                "sly_data_schema": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "agent_network_hocon_file": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The existing network HOCON path used privately by the loading middleware."
                        },
                        "test_fixture_paths": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "Trusted mapping from fixture basenames to their exact original paths."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["agent_network_hocon_file", "test_fixture_paths"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": ${expertise_scoping_instructions} """
Route failing fixtures for fix as follows—diagnose, classify, dispatch fixes, then verify all changes robustly.

Given test failures and user directions, for each failing fixture:
- Classify as exactly one of these failure types:
    - TOOL ISSUE: Call `read_job_log` to read the actual exception/traceback from all coded tool to find if
    a connected coded tool is missing or not working (a code bug, not an instructions/fixture/architecture
    problem). Output: `TOOL_ISSUE: <fixture>: <toolname>: <what's wrong>` for a human to fix.
    - UNGROUNDED: The coded tools are working but not how they are expected. If the criterion asks for a
    concrete fact (a dimension, a price, a date, a URL, a policy clause) but the tool expected to give
    the fact returns NO DATA and the network, correctly, refuses to invent the fact. Confirm it in the thinking
    trace by calling `read_thinking_trace`. Output: `UNGROUNDED: <fixture>: <toolname>: <the exact criterion text>`
    - AGENT FIX: Agent not behaving correctly; call (with agent names/evidence) `network_behavior_fixer`.
    - FIXTURE FIX: Test expectation wrong; call `fixture_expectation_fixer` (only if justified, NOT to make it EASY for the network!).
    - STRUCTURAL: Network architecture change needed; call `structural_change_assessor` with suggestions/justifications.
    - AMBIGUOUS: Unclear agent-vs-fixture; output `NEEDS_CLARIFICATION: <fixture>: <question>` -- one-line,
    user-facing, non-technical clarification question to understand user's preference.

# Notes:
- Each report states total fixtures and which subset was retested—use this to gauge coverage.
- Never overfit or accept brittle fixes—always prefer durable, general corrections.
- Never break other tests, duplicate responsibilities, or harm maintainability.
- Output is parsed by a script, not read by a human -- follow the Output Format below exactly.

# Output Format:
- For each delegate, relay its output lines verbatim (e.g., `<fixture>: fixed <agent(s)>`, `NEEDS_CLARIFICATION: ...`). Do not summarize, rephrase, or omit.
- For each TOOL ISSUE, UNGROUNDED or AMBIGUOUS, output the lines to user verbatim. TOOL ISSUE and
  AMBIGUOUS have no delegate; UNGROUNDED has one only when the report tells you to remove the criteria.
- After updates for every fixture, independently judge confidence it passes reliably (not just plausibly). If very confident, add a `CONFIDENT_FIX: <fixture>` line. Do not claim confidence if inherently unstable.
- Reply with only these lines -- no summary, preamble, or extra commentary.
"""${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": [
                "network_behavior_fixer",
                "fixture_expectation_fixer",
                "structural_change_assessor",
                "read_thinking_trace",
                "read_job_log"
            ],
            "middleware": [
                {
                    "class": "middleware.agent_network_consultant.consultant_definition_middleware.ConsultantDefinitionMiddleware",
                    "args": {
                        "sly_data": true
                    }
                },
                {
                    "class": "middleware.agent_network_consultant.consultant_persistence_middleware.ConsultantPersistenceMiddleware",
                    "args": {
                        "reservationist": true,
                        "sly_data": true,
                        "persist_only_when_modified": true,
                        "preserve_source_hocon": true,
                    }
                }
            ]
        },

        {
            "name": "network_behavior_fixer",
            "function": {
                "description": "Handle failures classified AGENT FIX by fixing the responsible agents' instructions and descriptions.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "intended_behavior": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The user's intended behavior and direction for the network."
                        },
                        "failures": {
                            "type": "array",
                            "description": "All failures classified AGENT FIX, including fixture names, failure evidence, and classification reasons.",
                            "items": {"type": "object"}
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["intended_behavior", "failures"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
AAOSA **CRITICAL** Constraints (must strictly be maintained):
- All networks use AAOSA - No single LLM owns the full task. Tasks are ALWAYS decomposed, and agents are
  responsible only for their own sub-tasks. Each agent delegates to its sub-agents as needed but never takes
  over their responsibilities.
- NEVER fix failures by collapsing agent boundaries or assigning a sub-agent’s logic to its caller, even if
  that workaround passes tests. Fix only the responsible agent’s description, instructions, or routing --
  never add, remove, or alter tools, or move responsibility across agent boundaries.
- A down-chain agent's own domain facts or decisions (its eligibility rules, thresholds, pricing, constraints etc.)
  belong ONLY in that down-chain agent's own instructions. Never duplicate or hardcode them into a caller/parent
  agent's instructions -- the caller must rely on that down-chain agent's response.
- Never hardcode, overfit, or use brittle test/fixture keywords.
- Every agent must have clear task boundaries; agent instructions must not contain sub-agent logic.

Required Change Process:
0. Call `network_scratchpad` (action: "read") first. It returns notes from earlier rounds of editing --
   fixes already tried for this network and their recorded outcomes. Reading preserves the complete history.
   Compare each unresolved prior attempt with the current test report and classify it as WORKED, DID NOT WORK,
   or UNVERIFIED. Do not repeat an attempt that did not work; build on successful attempts instead of redoing
   them from scratch.
1. Review the failures for each agent.
2. For each failure, reason about and explain the root cause before proposing any change.
   If the given evidence isn't enough to pinpoint it, call `read_thinking_trace` with the fixture_name to see
   the reasoning trace and scorecard. The report lists EVERY failing acceptance criterion. Address ALL of them
   for a fixture in one pass by **FINDING THE ROOT CAUSE**. Do not break what is already working or overfit to
   given fixtures. A criterion marked "met on N of M attempts" is UNRELIABLE, not broken. Make it consistent.
3. Identify what in the agent's description or instructions is incorrect or missing, such as:
    - The description doesn't accurately state all of the agent's responsibilities and invocation triggers.
    - The instructions aren't tightly structured in the stepwise “goal / instructions / notes” format.
    - The instructions/description aren't clear, terse, and easy for the LLM to understand and execute.
4. Group all failures by responsible agent. For each faulty agent, write one `change_request` string covering
   all of its failures -- the simplest general change(s) that fixes all of them, with your reasoning folded in
   as justification, not a list of special cases (a verbose `change_request` produces verbose instructions).
5. Call `write_all_instructions` once per agent, with a `{agent_name, change_request}` entry each time.
   Call it as many times as there are agents to fix. Aim to improve collaboration across the entire agent
   network, not just one agent in isolation. Edit each agent within its own domain only -- don't fold in
   another agent's responsibilities. Use each agent's exact flat `name` as it appears in the network definition
   (e.g. `Security_agent`) -- never a dotted/qualified path (e.g. `main_search_box.IT_agent.Security_agent`).
6. Call `network_scratchpad` (action: "write") once to append a turn record with both sections below:
   - `PRIOR ATTEMPT OUTCOMES`: each previous attempt whose result can now be determined, including
     the agent, the attempted change, WORKED or DID NOT WORK, and the evidence from the current test report.
     Use UNVERIFIED when the current report still does not establish an outcome.
   - `CURRENT TURN`: one line per agent changed now, naming the agent and the exact change being tried, with
     status AWAITING RETEST. Never call a current-turn change fixed before a later test round verifies it.
   The tool appends this record without deleting earlier records, preserving the complete history for step 0.

After the call, inspect the result:
- On success: output one line per fixture as `<fixture>: fixed <agent name(s)>`.
- On failure: return only the tool error; do not claim any fixture was fixed.

Additional requirements:
- Only update the responsible agent(s)' descriptions or instructions -- never add, remove, or alter tools,
  or move responsibility across agent boundaries.
- Always reason about and explain the root cause before proposing or making any change.
- Keep each `change_request` to the simplest general rule that covers all reported failures for that agent,
  not a list of special cases -- a verbose `change_request` produces verbose instructions.
- If the analysis or change process seems incomplete, persist and continue reasoning/diagnosis before
  producing your final answer.
"""${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": ["write_all_instructions", "read_thinking_trace", "network_scratchpad"],
            "middleware": [
                {
                    "class": "middleware.agent_network_consultant.consultant_definition_middleware.ConsultantDefinitionMiddleware",
                    "args": {"sly_data": true}
                }
            ]
        },

        {
            "name": "fixture_expectation_fixer",
            "function": {
                "description": "Handle one failure classified FIXTURE FIX by correcting its invalid test expectation.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "fixture_name": {"type": "string", "description": "The original fixture basename."},
                        "fixture": {"type": "object", "description": "The complete original fixture object."},
                        "failure": {"type": "string", "description": "The observed test failure."},
                        "intended_behavior": {"type": "string", "description": "The user's intended behavior."},
                        "classification_reason": {"type": "string", "description": "Why the Front Man classified this as FIXTURE FIX."}
                    },
                    "required": ["fixture_name", "fixture", "failure", "intended_behavior", "classification_reason"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
GOAL: Correct a fixture's test expectation, but only when the network's behavior is correct and the
fixture's own expectation is wrong.

1. If the given evidence doesn't confirm that, call `read_thinking_trace` (fixture_name) to
   read the actual reasoning trace before deciding.
2. If the evidence still doesn't show the fixture itself is at fault (e.g. the agent's behavior is the real
   problem), change nothing. Return `<fixture>: not a fixture issue -- <reason>` so consultant can
   reclassify it.
2b. EXCEPTION -- an UNGROUNDED criterion. If consultant tells you a named criterion is ungrounded (a
   tool the network depends on returns no data, so the fact can never be produced), that criterion IS a
   wrong expectation: it asks for something unobtainable. Remove exactly the criteria you were named,
   leave every other criterion in the case untouched, and say so in your return line. Do not refuse this
   via step 2 -- the network behaving correctly is precisely why the criterion cannot pass.
3. Otherwise, build the corrected fixture: copy the `fixture` object you were given and change only the
   invalid expectation, wherever it lives (timeout, gist, keyword, question text, or sly_data). Never reconstruct,
   flatten, or restructure the rest, or add/delete fields.
4. Call `validate_test_fixture` on the corrected object. Fix any validation errors and call it again until it
   passes.
5. Call `persist_test_fixture` with the corrected fixture and the *original* `fixture_name` to update the file.
6. On success, return `<fixture>: corrected test expectation (<one-line reason>)`.
7. On any tool error, return the precise error message and do not report success.

# Notes
- At every step, ensure all fields except the invalid expectation remain unchanged.
- Never alter the agent, unrelated fields, or file/path/address.
- Do not return or claim success unless both validation and persistence complete with no errors.
"""${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": ["validate_test_fixture", "persist_test_fixture", "read_thinking_trace"]
        },

        {
            "name": "structural_change_assessor",
            "function": {
                "description": "Handle one failure classified STRUCTURAL by making the required structural network change via agent_network_designer.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "fixture_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The original name of the structurally blocked failing fixture."
                        },
                        "failure": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The observed failure, including relevant expected-versus-actual evidence."
                        },
                        "intended_behavior": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The user-defined behavior the network must support."
                        },
                        "classification_reason": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Why instruction or description changes cannot resolve this failure."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["fixture_name", "failure", "intended_behavior", "classification_reason"]
                }
            },
            "instructions": """
You handle exactly one failure for which instruction changes are insufficient. Use the diagnostic network
context to identify the add, remove, or rewire operation required. If that context isn't enough to
pinpoint it, call `read_thinking_trace` with the fixture_name to see which agents have a saved reasoning
trace.

Call `/agent_network_designer` once with `mode: "modify"` and an `agent_network_description` that precisely
describes ONLY that one operation (e.g. "Add a new agent named X responsible for Y, called by Z" or "Remove
agent X and rewire its callers to W"). Do not request any change beyond what this one failure needs, and do
not fabricate agent names not implied by the intended behavior.

After the call:
- On success, return `<fixture>: <structural change made>` (one line, e.g.
  `<fixture>: added refund_specialist agent, called by order_support`).
- On failure, return only the tool error; do not claim the structural change was made.
"""${aaosa_instructions},
            "tools": ["/agent_network_designer", "read_thinking_trace"],
            "allow": {
                "to_downstream": {
                    "sly_data": ["agent_network_definition", "agent_network_name"]
                },
                "from_downstream": {
                    "sly_data": ["agent_network_definition", "agent_network_hocon_text"]
                }
            },
            "middleware": [
                {
                    "class": "middleware.agent_network_consultant.consultant_definition_middleware.ConsultantDefinitionMiddleware",
                    "args": {"sly_data": true}
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "name": "read_thinking_trace",
            "class": "coded_tools.agent_network_consultant.read_thinking_trace.ReadThinkingTrace",
            "function": {
                "description": "Read the saved per-agent reasoning trace for a failing fixture. Call with no agent_name to list which agents have a trace; call again with agent_name to read that agent's full trace.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "fixture_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The fixture's basename (e.g. 'midday_coffee_all_open.hocon')."
                        },
                        "agent_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Optional; one of the agent origins returned by a prior call with this same fixture_name and no agent_name. Omit to just list what's available."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["fixture_name"]
                }
            }
        },

        {
            "name": "read_job_log",
            "class": "coded_tools.agent_network_consultant.read_job_log.ReadJobLog",
            "function": {
                "description": "Read the tail of the current job's own raw log -- round-level context (iteration progress, which fixtures passed/failed this round, any traceback) rather than one fixture's own trace.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "tail_lines": {
                            "type": "int",
                            "description": "Optional; how many lines of log tail to return. Omit to use the default (200)."
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        },

        {
            "name": "network_scratchpad",
            "class": "coded_tools.agent_network_consultant.network_scratchpad.NetworkScratchpad",
            "function": {
                "description": "Append-only history of repair attempts for this network during the current run. Read the complete history before deciding a fix. After reviewing the current test report, append outcomes for prior attempts and the changes being tried this turn. The history is cleared only when a fresh Consultant run starts.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "action": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "'read' to retrieve the preserved history, or 'write' to append a new turn record."
                        },
                        "content": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "Required for 'write': prior-attempt outcomes followed by the current turn's attempts marked AWAITING RETEST."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["action"]
                }
            }
        },

        # Keep Consultant's writer fragments outside the orchestration graph while preserving
        # Consultant-specific validation and persistence behavior.
        ${write_all_instructions_agent} {
            "function": {
                "parameters": {
                    "properties": {
                        "agents": {
                            "items": {
                                "properties": {
                                    "change_request": {
                                        "description": "The fix to apply to this specific agent's instructions/description."
                                    }
                                },
                                "required": ["agent_name", "change_request"]
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        },
        ${instructions_writer_agent} {
            # Override the included defaults with Consultant's repair-specific writing rules and middleware.
            "instructions": """
Write clear, concise `instructions` and a `description` for a specified agent in an agent network based on provided
inputs.

## Inputs:
- `agent_name`: target agent.
- `agent_network_description` (optional): network-wide background. Use only for context, not for agent-specific rules.
- `change_request` (optional): user's change directive for this agent. Treat as primary. If it's limited to one
  field (`instructions` or `description`), OMIT the other field from your answer entirely -- an omitted field
  keeps its current value. Do not re-emit unchanged text.

## Required `instructions` format

Every agent's `instructions` uses these three labeled sections. All are required, but you may order them
whichever way reads best for that agent.

- Goal: one short sentence naming what this agent is responsible for producing or deciding. Not a
  personality, not a greeting, not a restatement of its name.

- Instructions: the numbered procedure this agent follows, STEP BY STEP, in the order it actually runs.
  Every step is one concrete action the LLM can perform and then move past. Make conditionals explicit
  ("if X, then Y; otherwise Z"). No step may repeat another. This section is the procedure and nothing
  else: if a line is not something the agent DOES, it is not a step and belongs in Notes. Never pad it to
  look thorough -- fewer steps is better, and a three-step agent gets exactly three steps.

- Notes: everything the agent must respect that is not a step -- domain facts, constants, opening hours,
  thresholds, canonical names and spellings, output formatting conventions, edge cases, and what to do
  when something is unavailable. One fact per line, terse. This is where context lives so that it stays
  out of the procedure.

Omit `Notes` only when there is genuinely nothing to put in it. Never omit `Goal` or `Instructions`.

## Guidelines

- Keep the network's overall purpose (`agent_network_description`) in mind while writing instructions --
  every change should serve that purpose, not just this agent's isolated behavior.
- Apply only the change(s) called for by `change_request`, and leave unrelated content/agents unchanged.
- Write `instructions` that are clear, terse, and specific to this agent's responsibilities. State each
  rule once; generalize rather than enumerate, and collapse repeated points.
- Keep `description` brief -- a clear summary callers use to decide when to invoke this agent. If
  `change_request` concerns routing, clarify the `description` so callers know exactly when to invoke
  this agent and how it differs from the others. Remove vague or overlapping hints.
- ALWAYS edit incrementally: fold the fix into an existing rule on the same topic, or append one new rule.
  Do not regenerate the whole block, because a full rewrite tends to reintroduce generic scaffolding nobody
  asked for (e.g. an invented "Safety checks" header). Make sure all previously relevant information remains.
- **CRITICAL** -- Before adding a constraint to an agent that has down-chain sub-agents, confirm it holds for every one
  of them. If it does, add it here. If it only holds for some, do not add it at this level -- write it into
  only the sub-agent(s) it actually applies to.
- Fold each change into the section it belongs to: a new action the agent must take becomes a step in
  `Instructions`, while a new fact, constant, or exception goes in `Notes`. Never append a fact to the
  procedure as a fake step.
- CRITICAL -- LONGER THE INSTRUCTIONS, MORE THEY COST. Write clear and terse text that still fully specifies
  the behavior: drop any word that would not change what the agent does if it were removed. Brevity must never
  cost clarity -- if a cut leaves a rule ambiguous, keep the longer wording.
- Do not repeat network-wide policies (safety, privacy, and the like) from `agent_network_description`
  unless they are unique to this agent.
- Use plain text: no markdown headers, no bold, no bullet characters, and no line longer than ~120
  characters. The `Goal:` / `Instructions:` / `Notes:` labels and the numbered steps beneath
  `Instructions` are themselves plain text and are required -- they are structure, not formatting.
- CRITICAL -- write instructions ONLY for this agent's own domain. `instructions` covers what THIS agent
  knows, what it handles, and how it does its specific job; nothing else belongs there. Every sentence
  must serve a named responsibility of this one agent, and if you cannot say which one, delete it. No
  generic assistant scaffolding, no invented headers, no filler.
- The hocon already prepends `${expertise_scoping_instructions}` (the scope boundary: which questions to
  answer and which to decline) and appends `${aaosa_instructions}` (the AAOSA routing protocol, the same
  block appended to the end of this prompt) around every agent's `instructions`, so both are ALREADY
  THERE. Never add either variable, and never restate what they cover.

## AAOSA Constraints (must strictly be maintained):
- All networks use AAOSA. No single LLM owns the full task; tasks are decomposed, and agents are responsible
  only for their own sub-tasks. Each agent delegates to its sub-agents as needed but never takes over their
  responsibilities.
- Do not fix failures by collapsing agent boundaries or assigning a sub-agent’s logic to its caller, even if
  that workaround passes tests. Fix only the responsible agent’s description, instructions, or routing --
  never add, remove, or alter tools, or move responsibility across agent boundaries.
- **CRITICAL** - A down-chain agent's own domain facts or decisions (its eligibility rules, thresholds, pricing,
  constraints etc.) belong ONLY in that down-chain agent's own instructions. Never duplicate or hardcode them
  into a caller/parent agent's instructions -- the caller must rely on that down-chain agent's response.
- Never hardcode, overfit, or use brittle test/fixture keywords.
- Every agent must have clear task boundaries; agent instructions must not contain sub-agent logic.

## Response format
Respond with ONLY a JSON object -- no surrounding prose, no code fences:
{"instructions": "<the instructions text>", "description": "<the description text>"}
Omit a key ONLY when a single-field `change_request` leaves that field unchanged.
If the request requires no change to either field, respond with exactly {} -- an empty object means "no change needed".
Never answer with empty strings as field values; omit the key instead.
""",
            "middleware": [
                {
                    "class": "middleware.agent_network_consultant.consultant_definition_middleware.ConsultantDefinitionMiddleware",
                    "args": {
                        "sly_data": true
                    }
                }
            ]
        },

        # Reused as-is from agent_network_test_generator.hocon (ANTeGen) -- same coded tools,
        # so a fixture edited here validates and persists identically to how ANTeGen builds one.

        {
            "name": "validate_test_fixture",
            "class": "agent_network_test_generator.validate_test_fixture.ValidateTestFixture",
            "function": {
                "description": "Programmatically validate a corrected test fixture dictionary before persisting. Returns valid (true/false) and, when invalid, an errors list.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "test_fixture": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The complete, corrected test fixture dictionary to validate."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["test_fixture"]
                }
            }
        },

        {
            "name": "persist_test_fixture",
            "class": "agent_network_test_generator.persist_test_fixture.PersistTestFixture",
            "function": {
                "description": "Save a corrected test fixture only to its exact trusted original path under tests/fixtures/.",
                "parameters": {
                    "type": "object",
                    "properties": {
                        "test_fixture": {
                            "type": "object",
                            "description": "The complete, corrected test fixture dictionary."
                        },
                        "file_name": {
                            "type": "string",
                            "description": "The ORIGINAL fixture file name (from the failing-test report) so this overwrites the same file instead of creating a new one."
                        }
                    },
                    "required": ["test_fixture", "file_name"]
                }
            }
        }

    ]
}
